AMDGPU: dynamic LDS is 16-byte aligned where LDS accesses must be aligned

Generic (subtargets without unaligned DS access: gfx8 and older; gfx803 is
where it was found and tested).

Verified on an RX 590 (gfx803) with clang built from ROCm llvm-project
rocm-6.4.4 (d366fa84) with this patch on top of 0001 and 0002: the repro
below and a variant that reaches the dynamic buffer through a non-inlined
function are right (shmem at byte 16), gfx900 output is unchanged (byte 2),
and PyTorch rebuilt with it returns torch.mode of short fp16 slices
correctly, with the GEMM shim bit-identical (46 of 46), PyTorch's OpInfo
comparison with the CPU unchanged (584 pass), the SD 1.5 UNet step and LoRA
training at the same speed, and no GPU faults.

WHY
---
Dynamic LDS (`extern __shared__ char shmem[]`) starts right after a
kernel's static LDS, aligned only to the dynamic variable's own alignment,
1 for a char array. CUDA aligns dynamic shared memory to 16 bytes, and
CUDA code relies on it: it declares the buffer as char and casts it to
wider types. On gfx9 and later unaligned DS accesses work, so the cast is
harmless there. gfx8 has no unaligned DS access: a ds_write_b32 or
ds_read_b32 at an address that is not a multiple of 4 uses the address
rounded down, and reads or overwrites the bytes before it.

PyTorch's torch.mode (TensorModeKernel.cuh, compute_mode) keeps the mode in
a static `__shared__ T mode` and later reuses the dynamic buffer as
`unsigned` scratch. For fp16, `mode` is the kernel's only static LDS (2
bytes) and the dynamic buffer starts at byte 2, so the scratch store at byte
2 lands on bytes 0-3 and replaces the mode with the low half of an index:
torch.mode of [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 0.5] in fp16 on the GPU returns
2.98e-07 (bit pattern 0x0005, the index of the last 3) instead of 3. fp32 is
right, because there the static size is 4. The same code compiled for
gfx900 has the same layout and the same ds_write_b32 at byte 2.

Repro outside PyTorch (hipcc -O2 --offload-arch=gfx803):

  template <typename T> __global__ void k(const T* in, T* out) {
      extern __shared__ char shmem[];
      T* smem = reinterpret_cast<T*>(shmem);
      smem[threadIdx.x] = in[threadIdx.x]; __syncthreads();
      __shared__ T mode;
      if (threadIdx.x == 0) mode = smem[3];
      __syncthreads();
      if (threadIdx.x < 16) reinterpret_cast<unsigned*>(shmem)[threadIdx.x] = 1000u + threadIdx.x;
      __syncthreads();
      if (threadIdx.x == 0) out[0] = mode; }

With T = __half the shipped compiler puts shmem at LDS byte 2 and out[0] is
5.96e-05 (0x03E8, the low half of 1000); declared
`extern __shared__ __attribute__((aligned(16))) char shmem[]` it is at byte
16 and out[0] is the expected value.

WHAT
----
On subtargets without unaligned DS access, dynamic LDS gets an alignment of
at least 16: AMDGPUMachineFunction::setDynLDSAlign raises the alignment it
pads the static size to (SIMachineFunctionInfo sets the minimum from the
subtarget), and AMDGPULowerModuleLDS gives the representative dynamic LDS
variable it creates for kernels that reach dynamic LDS through functions
the same minimum, so the address it records matches the one instruction
selection computes. A kernel's static LDS size grows by at most 15 bytes.
Subtargets with unaligned DS access are unchanged.

diff -ru a/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPULowerModuleLDSPass.cpp b/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPULowerModuleLDSPass.cpp
--- a/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPULowerModuleLDSPass.cpp	2026-10-25 17:24:30.296491852 -0700
+++ b/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPULowerModuleLDSPass.cpp	2026-10-25 17:24:30.307495984 -0700
@@ -179,6 +179,7 @@
 #include "AMDGPU.h"
 #include "AMDGPUMemoryUtils.h"
 #include "AMDGPUTargetMachine.h"
+#include "GCNSubtarget.h"
 #include "Utils/AMDGPUBaseInfo.h"
 #include "llvm/ADT/BitVector.h"
 #include "llvm/ADT/DenseMap.h"
@@ -819,7 +820,7 @@
 
   static GlobalVariable *
   buildRepresentativeDynamicLDSInstance(Module &M, LDSUsesInfoTy &LDSUsesInfo,
-                                        Function *func) {
+                                        Function *func, Align MinAlign) {
     // Create a dynamic lds variable with a name associated with the passed
     // function that has the maximum alignment of any dynamic lds variable
     // reachable from this kernel. Dynamic LDS is allocated after the static LDS
@@ -834,7 +835,10 @@
 
     LLVMContext &Ctx = M.getContext();
     const DataLayout &DL = M.getDataLayout();
-    Align MaxDynamicAlignment(1);
+    // At least the alignment AMDGPUMachineFunction::setDynLDSAlign gives the
+    // dynamic LDS of a kernel on this subtarget, so that the address recorded
+    // here is the one instruction selection computes.
+    Align MaxDynamicAlignment = MinAlign;
 
     auto UpdateMaxAlignment = [&MaxDynamicAlignment, &DL](GlobalVariable *GV) {
       if (AMDGPU::isDynamicLDS(*GV)) {
@@ -885,8 +889,12 @@
             report_fatal_error("Anonymous kernels cannot use LDS variables");
           }
 
-          GlobalVariable *N =
-              buildRepresentativeDynamicLDSInstance(M, LDSUsesInfo, func);
+          const Align MinAlign =
+              TM.getSubtarget<GCNSubtarget>(*func).hasUnalignedDSAccessEnabled()
+                  ? Align(1)
+                  : Align(16);
+          GlobalVariable *N = buildRepresentativeDynamicLDSInstance(
+              M, LDSUsesInfo, func, MinAlign);
 
           KernelToCreatedDynamicLDS[func] = N;
 
diff -ru a/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUMachineFunction.cpp b/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUMachineFunction.cpp
--- a/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUMachineFunction.cpp	2026-10-25 17:24:30.293098714 -0700
+++ b/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUMachineFunction.cpp	2026-10-25 17:24:30.307184675 -0700
@@ -203,8 +203,9 @@
   const DataLayout &DL = M->getDataLayout();
   assert(DL.getTypeAllocSize(GV.getValueType()).isZero());
 
-  Align Alignment =
-      DL.getValueOrABITypeAlignment(GV.getAlign(), GV.getValueType());
+  Align Alignment = std::max(
+      MinDynLDSAlign,
+      DL.getValueOrABITypeAlignment(GV.getAlign(), GV.getValueType()));
   if (Alignment <= DynLDSAlign)
     return;
 
diff -ru a/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUMachineFunction.h b/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUMachineFunction.h
--- a/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUMachineFunction.h	2026-10-25 17:24:30.290732132 -0700
+++ b/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUMachineFunction.h	2026-10-25 17:24:30.307115396 -0700
@@ -46,6 +46,12 @@
   /// stages.
   Align DynLDSAlign;
 
+  /// Least alignment of dynamic shared memory. Code written for CUDA relies on
+  /// dynamic shared memory being 16-byte aligned and casts it to wider types;
+  /// where LDS accesses must be aligned (gfx8 and older), an access through
+  /// such a cast at a smaller alignment hits the aligned-down address.
+  Align MinDynLDSAlign;
+
   // Flag to check dynamic LDS usage by kernel.
   bool UsesDynamicLDS = false;
 
diff -ru a/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/SIMachineFunctionInfo.cpp b/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/SIMachineFunctionInfo.cpp
--- a/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/SIMachineFunctionInfo.cpp	2026-10-25 17:24:30.294899268 -0700
+++ b/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/SIMachineFunctionInfo.cpp	2026-10-25 17:24:30.307287725 -0700
@@ -44,6 +44,8 @@
       WorkItemIDY(false), WorkItemIDZ(false), ImplicitArgPtr(false),
       GITPtrHigh(0xffffffff), HighBitsOf32BitAddress(0) {
   const GCNSubtarget &ST = *static_cast<const GCNSubtarget *>(STI);
+  if (!ST.hasUnalignedDSAccessEnabled())
+    MinDynLDSAlign = Align(16);
   FlatWorkGroupSizes = ST.getFlatWorkGroupSizes(F);
   WavesPerEU = ST.getWavesPerEU(F);
   MaxNumWorkGroups = ST.getMaxNumWorkGroups(F);
